// wraps NEGATIVE -> mid < 0 -> ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`,question:"Safe formula?",options:["lo + (hi - lo) / 2","(lo + hi) % 2","hi - lo","lo >>> 1"],correct:0,explanation:"The sum overflows int and wraps negative even though BOTH operands and the true midpoint fit comfortably. lo + (hi-lo)/2 never forms the big sum: the difference always fits. This exact bug sat in java.util.Arrays.binarySearch until 2006 — proof that it survives review. % 2 gives a parity bit, hi - lo is a width, and lo >>> 1 halves the wrong thing.",hint:"Both lo and hi fit in an int. What intermediate value does not?"},{type:"scramble",prompt:"Assemble the iterative binary search",pieces:[{id:"a",text:"static int bsearch(int[] arr, int t) {"},{id:"b",text:"    int lo = 0, hi = arr.length - 1;"},{id:"c",text:"    while (lo <= hi) {"},{id:"d",text:"        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;"},{id:"e",text:"        if (arr[mid] == t) return mid;"},{id:"f",text:"        else if (arr[mid] < t) lo = mid + 1;"},{id:"g",text:"        else hi = mid - 1;"},{id:"h",text:"    }"},{id:"i",text:"    return -1;"},{id:"j",text:"}"}],distractors:[{id:"k",text:"        lo = mid;"}],correctOrder:["a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h","i","j"],explanation:"The equality check must come before the two moves, and both moves must EXCLUDE mid — it was just examined. The spare line lo = mid is the classic infinite loop: on a two-element window mid equals lo, so nothing changes. And return -1 sits after the loop, reachable only when the window has closed empty.",hint:"Each branch must shrink the window. Which piece fails to?"}],23:[{type:"trace",prompt:"How does the work grow when n doubles?",code:[{text:"for (int i=0; i<n; i++)"},{text:"    for (int j=0; j<n; j++)"},{text:'        System.out.println(i+","+j);'}],options:["O(n)","O(n²)","O(n log n)","O(2ⁿ)"],correct:1,explanation:"The inner loop runs n times for each of the n outer passes — n × n = n². The practical meaning: double n and the work quadruples. O(n log n) would need the inner range to shrink or halve; O(2ⁿ) is a different universe, where adding ONE to n doubles the work.",hint:"Count total prints for n=10, then for n=20. What is the ratio?"},{type:"pair",prompt:"Match each lookup to its cost",pairs:[{id:1,left:"HashMap.get()",right:"O(1) amortized"},{id:2,left:"TreeMap.get()",right:"O(log n)"},{id:3,left:"ArrayList.get(i)",right:"O(1)"},{id:4,left:"LinkedList.get(i)",right:"O(n)"}],explanation:"The pair worth remembering is the last two: both are Lists with the same interface, but get(i) on a LinkedList walks the chain from the nearest end every single call. Loop over indices on a LinkedList and an innocent-looking O(n) loop silently becomes O(n²). TreeMap pays log n for what HashMap does in O(1) — its purchase is sorted order and range queries.",hint:"Same interface does not mean same cost. Which one has to walk to reach index i?"},{type:"trace",prompt:"i is halved each pass. What is the complexity?",code:[{text:"int i = n;"},{text:"while (i > 0) {"},{text:"    System.out.println(i);"},{text:"    i = i / 2;",highlight:!0},{text:"}"}],options:["O(n)","O(log n)","O(n²)","O(1)"],correct:1,explanation:"The loop variable is divided, not decremented — that single character difference is the whole answer. From n it takes log₂(n) halvings to reach 0, and integer division is what guarantees the loop terminates: 1/2 is exactly 0 in int arithmetic, no floor call needed.",hint:"Look only at the highlighted line. Is i shrinking by a constant amount, or by a constant factor?"},{type:"refactor",prompt:"The check runs thousands of times. Which rewrite is both correct and fast?",originalCode:`// runs in a hot loop:
